  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 142 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nAs mentioned, in the previous discussion, the Extreme Heat\nWarning will continue through Monday night due to heat index\nvalues above 110 degrees. Highs will be in the upper 90s to\nlower 100s, very similar to today. The dewpoints may be a bit\nlower by a degree or two tomorrow, but the confidence in that\nfactor bringing down heat index values enough to just go with an\nadvisory instead of a warning just wasn't there for us. Also,\nthe heat related impacts will be the same either way. As soon as\nthe Extreme Heat Warning expires on Tuesday morning, a Heat\nAdvisory will replace it and continue through 7 PM Tuesday.\nTuesday will be a bit trick on whether or not every county will\nstill need a Heat Advisory. This is due to a few models showing\na decaying system moving through our western counties on\nTuesday. This would likely bring cloud cover and hopefully\ncooler temperatures and heat index values that wouldn't meet \nadvisory criteria. However, not much rain is expected in our \narea due to dry air that will likely be in place. \n\nThe rest of the week looks a bit more seasonal for this time of\nyear with highs in the lower 90s and heat index values in the\n90s as well. There will be daily low-end rain chances through\nthe rest of the week as the ridge begins to shift slightly off\nto the west and the trough that's currently over New England\nbegins to dig and expand further west. This will allow for a few\nshortwaves to make it through the area and bring us 15-20% POPs.\nFriday is expected to be the best day for rain with POPs near\n20-50% as a stronger shortwave moves over the eastern portion of\nthe state. Areas east of Highway 65 have the best chance to see\naccumulating rainfall with amounts near 1\". With the rain also\ncomes a little cooldown with highs in the 80s going into this\nweekend.",
